%I A401204 #13 Nov 01 2026 20:46:07
%S A401204 12,384,12321,371712,10634460,292771602,7840133364,205687578624,
%T A401204 5312055930723,135495271297920,3421537009450692,85686875330918436,
%U A401204 2130987652848774876,52682956800761551488,1295799745792558468005,31730078000199948238848,773935208068919682371244
%N A401204 Number of spanning trees of the graph formed by n triangles arranged in a ring, with corresponding vertices of consecutive triangles joined and the ring closed by a cyclic shift of the vertices.
%C A401204 The graph has vertices (i,j) with 0 <= i < n and j in Z/3Z, and edges (i,j)-(i,j') for j != j', (i,j)-(i+1,j) for 0 <= i < n-1, and (n-1,j)-(0,j+1).
%C A401204 For n <= 2 parallel edges are kept and loops are discarded, the same convention as in A212798.
%C A401204 This is the twisted analog of C_n X K_3 (A212798). Closing the ring with a transposition of the vertices instead gives n*(A003501(n)^2-4)/3 spanning trees. For every n, A212798(n) < n*(A003501(n)^2-4)/3 < a(n), so the cyclic shift gives the most spanning trees among all ways of closing the ring with a permutation of the three vertices.
%C A401204 The formula follows from the Matrix-Tree Theorem: the Laplacian eigenvalues are 2 - 2*cos(2*Pi*m/n) and 5 - 2*cos(2*Pi*(m + r/3)/n) for r = 1, 2 and 0 <= m < n.
%C A401204 The formula was derived with the assistance of an AI system (Claude, Anthropic).
%H A401204 Andrew Howroyd, <a href="/A401204/b401204.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..500</a>
%H A401204 <a href="/index/Rec#order_10">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (58,-1131,8700,-29493,43734,-29493,8700,-1131, 58,-1).
%F A401204 a(n) = n*(A003501(n) + 1)^2/3.
%F A401204 a(n) = (n/3)*(1 + ((5+sqrt(21))/2)^n + ((5-sqrt(21))/2)^n)^2.
%F A401204 G.f.: 3*x*(4 - 104*x + 1207*x^2 - 4334*x^3 + 7777*x^4 - 4334*x^5 + 1207*x^6 - 104*x^7 + 4*x^8)/((1 - x)^2*(1 - 23*x + x^2)^2*(1 - 5*x + x^2)^2).
%e A401204 a(3) = 12321: A003501(3) = 110, so a(3) = 3*111^2/3 = 111^2 = 12321.
%o A401204 (Python)
%o A401204 def A401204_gen():  # generator of terms
%o A401204     a, b = 2, 5
%o A401204     n = 1
%o A401204     while True:
%o A401204         yield n*(b + 1)**2//3
%o A401204         a, b = b, 5*b - a
%o A401204         n += 1
%o A401204 (PARI) a(n) = n*(polchebyshev(n,1,5/2)*2 + 1)^2/3 \\ _Andrew Howroyd_, Oct 22 2026
%Y A401204 Cf. A003501, A020871, A212798.
%K A401204 nonn,easy,new
%O A401204 1,1
%A A401204 _Paul Mezy_, Oct 17 2026
